test_jacobian_scan_negative_control_can_fail

This is itself a negative control: it asserts that a deliberately
inconsistent Jacobian IS caught (mutating self.wall_recycling between the
analytic Jacobian capture and the finite-difference residual evaluations
must make best > FD_TOLERANCE). Its red state is therefore the INVERSE of
the usual cycle: make the residual path insensitive to the live mutation of
wall_recycling, so the inconsistency the test relies on stops existing and
its own "the scan can fail" assertion fails instead.

Breakage: rmgpy/solver/plasma.pyx. Added a frozen snapshot
wall_recycling_frozen, captured once in __init__ right after
self.wall_recycling is validated, and made _apply_wall_terms (the function
residual()/the FD scan actually exercises) read the frozen snapshot instead
of the live self.wall_recycling. _apply_wall_jacobian is untouched and still
reads self.wall_recycling live. Net effect: a post-construction mutation of
wall_recycling no longer changes what residual() computes, so the analytic
Jacobian (captured before the mutation, when wall_recycling was still at its
original value) and the finite difference (computed after the mutation, but
now still evaluating the frozen gamma) agree again.

